          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "The 'Symptoms, Signs and Abnormal Findings' chapter in medical coding (ICD-10-CM Chapter 18, codes R00-R99) is used to classify symptoms, signs, and abnormal clinical or laboratory findings when a definitive diagnosis has not yet been established.\n\nYou should use codes from this chapter when:\n*   A specific diagnosis has not been recorded, even after investigation.\n*   The signs and symptoms are transient and a cause is not identified.\n*   A provisional diagnosis is made, but further investigation is needed.\n\nFor example, abnormal blood or urine findings, chest pain, or an abnormal liver function test would be coded from Chapter 18 if there is no confirmed diagnosis. Once a definitive diagnosis is made, you would then use the disease-specific code from the relevant chapter, such as the endocrine chapter for diabetes or the circulatory system chapter for heart conditions.\n\nThis ensures that the patient's record accurately reflects the information available at the time of coding, without prematurely assigning a diagnosis.\n\nThis information comes from the \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00-R99)\" document, as well as contextual information from \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 4: endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases (E00-E89)\", \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99)\", \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system (K00-K95)\", and \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 10: diseases of the respiratory system (J00-J99)\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
